1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,500
This is episode two of The Synthetic Signal, and like episode one it is completely fabricated.

2
00:00:10,500 --> 00:00:24,000
Ada Kestrel here with Milo Vance. Every company, price and statistic in this episode was invented for a software demo.

3
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:42,500
Northwind Logistics reported an invented eleven percent drop in spot freight rates this quarter.

4
00:00:42,500 --> 00:01:04,000
Spot rates are what you pay when you did not book ahead, which in this made-up market is most of the time.

5
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:28,500
The invented CPI print for the quarter came in at two point four percent.

6
00:01:28,500 --> 00:01:54,000
Milo, does a fictional CPI print tell you anything at all about fictional freight?

7
00:01:54,000 --> 00:02:22,500
Not directly. Fuel is a much bigger input than the CPI basket we invented would suggest.

8
00:02:22,500 --> 00:02:53,000
Our made-up GDP figure grew one point one percent, which in this fantasy counts as a soft landing.

9
00:02:53,000 --> 00:03:25,500
Harbour Robotics turns up here too, because Northwind Logistics is a fictional customer of theirs.

10
00:03:25,500 --> 00:03:59,000
They bought two hundred imaginary picking arms and claim a thirty percent throughput gain.

11
00:03:59,000 --> 00:04:34,500
A claim like that is exactly the kind of thing you want a timestamp attached to.

12
00:04:34,500 --> 00:05:12,000
Meridian Grid, our invented utility, raised overnight power prices again in this storyline.

13
00:05:12,000 --> 00:05:52,500
That pushes Northwind Logistics towards daytime charging, which collides with their invented delivery windows.

14
00:05:52,500 --> 00:06:35,000
The AI scheduling system they lease is supposed to solve exactly that, in this fiction.

15
00:06:35,000 --> 00:07:20,500
It costs them a fictional nineteen dollars per GPU hour, billed monthly, and it is not clear it earns that back.

16
00:07:20,500 --> 00:08:08,000
That is the show. None of it is real, none of it is advice, and all of it carries a timestamp you can check.
